WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.440
This is the Technical Difficulties.
We're playing Citation Needed.

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:05.680
Joining me in the studio today:
He reads books, you know — it's Chris Joel!

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:07.300
[Lapses into gibberish]

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:09.440
Good start.
Everybody's favourite Gary Brannan — Gary Brannan!

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:10.800
Coherency!

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:12.320
And the bounciest man on the internet — Matt Gray!

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:13.880
Hello YouTube.

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:18.060
In front of me I have an article from Wikipedia,
and these folks can't see it.

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:20.460
Every fact they get right gets a point and a ding [DING],

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:22.840
and the special prize for particularly good answers,
which is...

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:29.320
Today's article is:
the flitch of bacon custom.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:30.320
[Laughter]

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:32.340
Oh yeah! Good luck with this one, guys.

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:33.840
Tell me that's not a person.

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:36.820
Tell me that's not a ceremonial position
in the House — in Parliament.

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:40.540
The Black Rod! The Flitch of Bacon Custom!

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:42.780
Oh, excuse me a second... got a flitch of bacon.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:45.620
The flitch of bacon...

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:47.620
Is it the custom of the flitch of bacon?

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:48.560
TOM: Yes it is.

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:51.180
Yeah. Is a flitch an amount, or cut, or volume of bacon?

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:52.180
Yes! Have a point. [DING]

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:53.480
A unit of bacon measure.

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:54.820
It's a fairly thick slab.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:55.740
Right...

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:58.100
It's basically what you cut up
to make thin rashers of bacon.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:00:58.860
CHRIS: Right!

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:02.320
Okay. So we're actually talking
proper bacon, and a size of.

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:03.280
TOM: Yes.

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:05.900
Is this something like Fish Slapping Dance?

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:07.680
ALL: Ohh!

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:09.240
With a side o' bacon...

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:10.220
Bacon beating!

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:11.780
Brannan, if you'd care to mime with me...

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:12.620
GARY: Go on.

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:15.360
Comme ça?

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:19.640
Entire pig.
Boom! Into the Thames.

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:23.260
Well actually, an entire pig would go, 'OEEEK!'
as it comes through there.

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:25.940
'Rodents of Unusual Size? I don't think they exist —'

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:26.720
BOOM!

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:28.420
Pigs do fly!

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:30.460
They do if you stick a jet engine on the top of 'em.

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:32.480
Science!
[Laughter]

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:37.640
'An experiment was carried out,
attaching a small jet engine to —'

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:43.140
I think that oversteps the boundaries
from technical mirthery to animal cruelty.

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:44.320
CHRIS: Pig murder.

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:45.720
Where is the boundary there?

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:47.920
I think it's the bit where you
strapped a jet engine to a pig.

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:50.500
To be fair, we could use a dead pig.

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:51.900
Just to test the concept.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:53.600
Wait, wait, wait.

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:57.020
Heat off jet engine — delicious, delicious bacon!

00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:59.760
I'm thinking, yeah, we wrap the outside of the pig

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:02.140
in some fuel that isn't as noxious as jet fuel,

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:04.520
so that when it inevitably crashes...

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:05.820
Boomf! Instant bacon.

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:07.640
Wait, crackling as well?

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:08.800
CHRIS: Instant barbecue, yeah.

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:10.640
GARY: The heat you get round the edge
of that'll sort the skin out!

00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:12.120
GARY and MATT: Ohhh.

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:14.300
We just need to sneak onto a runway at Heathrow,

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:16.960
with, I assume, like, a garden fork with a pig on the end,

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:18.760
and we need a military jet with an afterburner.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:20.640
I don't think we really need an afterburner...

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:22.280
We just need any kind of burner.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:24.360
A jet engine probably produces
enough heat without an afterburner.

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:25.980
Eh, I'm going for spectacle, to be honest.

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:29.040
The flitch of bacon custom, gentlemen.

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:32.000
Okay, we're talking Ye Olde, aren't we.

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:33.860
Um... how Ye Olde?

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:35.100
Medieval?

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:36.460
14th, 15th century?

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:37.720
Yeah, I'll give you that. [DING]

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:40.600
It can be traced back to at least the 14th century,
survived until about the 18th. Have a point.

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:42.140
Is it in this country?

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:43.260
TOM: Yes. Very much so.

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:46.500
I'm going to say it's one of the wackier parts
of this fine nation, am I right?

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:47.500
Yes.

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:51.260
Are we talking southern, the kind of place
where they throw cheese down a hill and chase it,

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:54.020
when, to be honest, you can go to
any old shop and find still cheese?

00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:57.700
It's not quite that violent.

00:02:57.800 --> 00:02:59.160
The thrill of the hunt, old man!
[Laughter]

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:01.460
It's f***ing cheese!

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:03.540
Who wants an Edam that doesn't fight back?

00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:06.320
I mean, there's someone out there,
bless you in Gloucestershire...

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:07.760
It is in Gloucestershire, isn't it?

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:09.060
It is in Gloucestershire, the cheese-rolling.

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:11.980
The cheese-rolling. Someone out there has
f***ed their ankle and goes into hospital,

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:14.440
and years later: 'How did you break your ankle, Daddy?'

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:16.820
'Well, I was chasing a giant Edam...' or whatever it is.

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:19.020
The Gloucestershire cheese-rolling,
for anyone who doesn't know,

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:21.760
is an event every spring in Gloucestershire

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:25.360
where they get a lot of people at the top
of a hill, chuck a roll of cheese down,

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:27.300
and then everyone just kind of rag-dolls after it.

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:30.000
They say you're meant to run down the hill,
after about ten seconds...

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:32.260
It's less of a run... it's more of a
windmill-cum-flailing, isn't it.

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:33.140
TOM: Yeah.

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:36.260
Who... who wants a placid, tasteless cheese,

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:39.840
when you can have one that's run wild
and free and developed true taste?

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:42.160
Do you do that all the time then?
Do you just go to a supermarket,

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:45.660
get a Müller Crunch Corner and whang it
down a hill because it tastes better?

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:47.080
It's how you mature them.

00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:48.480
CHRIS: Absolutely!
[Laughter]

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:51.660
By the way, the cheese-rolling thing,
for the last few years...

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:54.440
can you tell me why it's not quite been
as safe as it might have been?

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:56.160
Er... the land mines.

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:00.020
Yeah, just rolling land mines down.

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:01.540
GARY: There was a mix-up.

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:02.820
CHRIS: Due to cheese shortages.

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:03.940
Chutney explosions.

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:05.420
[Explosion sounds]

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:07.700
Was it still in the cow?

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:15.200
The answer is that it was official until about 2010.

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:18.520
Then it got banned on grounds for health and safety,
so people just turn up and do it anyway.

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:19.380
CHRIS: Wahey!

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:20.700
...only without as much medical support.

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:21.900
Oh s***!

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:23.840
Good on ya, Gloucestershians!

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:26.380
A long time ago, you asked whether it was down south.

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:28.020
And... well, sort of.

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:29.440
Is it bottom left? Bottom right?

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:31.540
There's references to it in Essex...

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:35.280
But there's also references to it
in Staffordshire, up near Burton-on-Trent.

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:37.100
So it's all in the South.

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:41.400
Thank you, Yorkshireman. Okay, yes.
By Yorkshire standards, it's in the South.

00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:44.740
The South... where they drink beer wrong
and the tea tastes funny.

00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:50.140
Is it that certain local yeomen of certain standing

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:53.100
must donate X much to the local lord

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:55.640
from the first cut of bacon of the year or...

00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:57.100
That's what I was going to go with.

00:04:57.280 --> 00:04:59.920
No, it's bacon that is given to someone.

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:03.820
And to be honest, there's only one person at this table
who would be eligible for it right now.

00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:05.780
Married man?

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:07.480
Married man and his partner, yes.

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:08.580
Hello!

00:05:08.740 --> 00:05:09.780
TOM: Have a point. [DING]

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:12.020
He did say you've got to be virtuous as well, mate.

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:14.360
[Laughter]
Hey, hey, hey, now...

00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:18.140
It's given a year and a day after the wedding...

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:19.020
GARY: Cool.

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:25.220
To a man and his wife, because this is
ancient times and it was a man and *his* wife...

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:26.960
GARY: Indeed, yes, yes...

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:29.920
...who can attest to something, after a year and a day.

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:31.560
That they've not 'done it'.

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:34.660
That they've done it every day!

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:37.800
That's grounds for annulment, not a celebration, Gary.

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:39.200
TOM: You're closer...

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:40.520
Without going over.

00:05:40.660 --> 00:05:41.980
MATT: They will produce children.

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:43.820
It's something to do with the marriage.
It's not to do with anything else.

00:05:43.820 --> 00:05:45.800
It's something they feel about the marriage.

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:47.340
Divorce?

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:49.060
No, quite the opposite.

00:05:49.180 --> 00:05:51.680
You get your marriage upgraded to a supermarriage.

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:53.360
That's all I can think of!

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:55.740
It kind of already is, at this point.

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:58.500
You would do it again.
You would recommend it to a friend.

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:00.120
You redo your vows!

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:02.000
I'm going to give Gary the point. [DING]

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:05.600
That you don't regret it. That you have
never regretted it in that year and a day.

00:06:06.740 --> 00:06:09.540
If you can testify that you —
I'm not going to ask you that question, Gary...

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:11.420
but if you can testify that you have never —

00:06:11.540 --> 00:06:13.860
Well, put it this way:
I am due me some bacon!

00:06:14.020 --> 00:06:14.700
[Laughter]

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:18.200
And the other thing is, it's going to get to day 364:

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:21.460
'Yeah, it's been pretty poor, but we want
this bacon, don't we love?'  'Yeah.'

00:06:22.060 --> 00:06:24.400
'But there is a bit of bacon on the way,
so how about we just...'

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:27.160
I think we can land the second year
on the back of the bacon.

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:28.120
GARY: Yeah.

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:32.360
The flitch of bacon was actually referred to
in a very famous book.

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:33.200
Chaucer.

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:35.020
Yeah, absolutely right. Referred to in Chaucer. [DING]

00:06:35.020 --> 00:06:39.020
'...in a way that makes clear the reference
would already be well known to the reader.'

00:06:39.140 --> 00:06:40.900
CHRIS: Ah, okay.
TOM: So this is pretty widespread.

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:43.880
Did he do a look down the camera and go,
'Y'know what I mean?'

00:06:44.060 --> 00:06:45.700
It's in the Wife of Bath's prologue.

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:47.180
GARY: Is that the one where the nun farts on them?

00:06:47.300 --> 00:06:49.140
Oh blimey, right. So he probably did
look down the camera and go,

00:06:49.260 --> 00:06:51.480
'If you know what I mean!'

00:06:51.640 --> 00:06:56.680
'The bacon was nat fet for hem, I trowe,
That som men han in Essex at Dunmowe.'

00:06:56.680 --> 00:06:58.020
Which is the place that...

00:06:58.140 --> 00:07:00.380
TOM: I'm mispronouncing that,
obviously, because Chaucer.

00:07:00.580 --> 00:07:02.240
Good effort, old boy. Good effort.

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:03.760
You probably did better than we did.

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:06.420
I'd like to point out that I know nothing about Chaucer.

00:07:06.420 --> 00:07:10.280
I just know, that's an old book,
and whenever I say 'Chaucer' I get it right.

00:07:10.420 --> 00:07:12.020
[Laughter]
Yeah. Point, by the way.

00:07:12.180 --> 00:07:13.700
CHRIS: Chaucer — point!

00:07:13.960 --> 00:07:16.760
'And thannen he gaven to him a pointe!'

00:07:17.900 --> 00:07:20.140
'And thereof pronounced a ding.'

00:07:20.140 --> 00:07:21.340
Ding!

00:07:21.540 --> 00:07:24.020
'A ding from table woode!'

00:07:24.500 --> 00:07:25.300
[Laughter]

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:28.400
'And Biscuits of Mysterie, for foode.'

00:07:30.700 --> 00:07:31.500
We got that.

00:07:32.660 --> 00:07:34.860
Right. Flitch trials, as they were called —

00:07:34.980 --> 00:07:38.300
'Welcome to the 7 O'Clock News. I'm Flitch Trials.'

00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:41.240
Is that like a witch trial?

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:42.900
'I accuse you of being bacon and
I'm going to have to burn you.'

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:45.040
The best thing is, he's pointing
at a pig when he says that.

00:07:45.820 --> 00:07:46.700
CHRIS: [Oinks]

00:07:46.700 --> 00:07:48.180
CHRIS: That was the worst pig...
GARY: [Oinks more realistically]

00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:49.980
Thank you. My pig impression was terrible.

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:51.960
TOM: Hold on! Gary Brannan impressions: Pig.
GARY: [Oinks]

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:53.380
TOM: Goose.
GARY: [Honks]

00:07:53.380 --> 00:07:54.760
TOM: Train.
GARY: [Two-tone horn]

00:07:54.860 --> 00:07:55.980
Goose on a train?

00:07:56.180 --> 00:07:57.940
[Horn and roaring engine]

00:07:58.340 --> 00:07:59.320
Thank you.

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:03.040
That's not getting old, for some reason.

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:07.700
I love the way that the goose is never just
sat on the train, it always ends up...

00:08:07.700 --> 00:08:09.460
...on the train.
[Laughter]

00:08:09.700 --> 00:08:11.860
Well, you said 'on'. You didn't say in what state.

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:13.840
There's a lovely old thing in linguistics,

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:16.120
about mass and count nouns,
the difference between them.

00:08:16.120 --> 00:08:20.480
'There was A goose on the train'
versus 'There was goose on the train'.

00:08:20.740 --> 00:08:21.560
Yes.

00:08:22.460 --> 00:08:25.480
The worst thing is, he's talking about
the copilot from Top Gun.

00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:34.140
What are you required to bring along
to a flitch trial to prove that —

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:35.700
Applesauce.

00:08:36.060 --> 00:08:36.940
Fair point.

00:08:37.560 --> 00:08:39.700
Better question: *Who* are you required to produce?

00:08:39.820 --> 00:08:40.640
Vicar.

00:08:40.760 --> 00:08:41.420
TOM: Not quite.

00:08:41.560 --> 00:08:42.400
Your mum.

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:45.580
Oh yeah. Your best man, or something like that.

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:47.520
MATT: Your wife.
CHRIS: Witnesses of some species.

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:48.440
Yup.

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:49.540
GARY: Neighbours.

00:08:49.640 --> 00:08:52.380
Point. [DING] Two of your neighbours
to prove that the oath is true.

00:08:52.480 --> 00:08:54.040
Yeah, prove that they haven't had any rows. Yeah.

00:08:54.140 --> 00:08:56.160
That't probably work quite well, actually.

00:08:56.500 --> 00:08:58.900
Well, in a lot of these kind of cases,

00:08:58.900 --> 00:09:01.500
working as I do with these kind of things to do with —

00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:03.340
Gonna say, can we get an archivist shout here?

00:09:03.460 --> 00:09:07.460
Yeah, in a lot of these kind of
non-consummation cases or adultery,

00:09:07.460 --> 00:09:09.540
a lot of the evidence comes from neighbours,

00:09:09.540 --> 00:09:12.900
who have obviously been peeping out the back window
and going, 'Ooh, that's not the missus.'

00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:16.060
So assuming you win the flitch of bacon...

00:09:16.060 --> 00:09:19.160
What happens? You get the bacon, and then...?

00:09:19.260 --> 00:09:20.720
Feast for friends and family!

00:09:20.820 --> 00:09:22.220
A grand ceremony. [DING]

00:09:22.220 --> 00:09:26.240
With 'trompets, tabourets,
and other manoir of mynstralcie'.

00:09:26.360 --> 00:09:29.580
That's what you'd expect, I'd imagine,
for that kind of size of thing?

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:31.620
But you'd be doing that a lot.

00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:34.780
I was going to say, there's a lot of
weddings we're talking about here.

00:09:34.780 --> 00:09:39.700
Have we just got pigs and pigs and pigs,
and minstrels knackered from playing every day?

00:09:39.800 --> 00:09:42.620
Well, this is the thing. It doesn't seem
to have been claimed all that often.

00:09:43.260 --> 00:09:46.120
Oh, it's one of these things that's on the books
but you've got to ask for it, right?

00:09:46.240 --> 00:09:47.220
TOM: Yup.
CHRIS: Yeah.

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:50.760
How many times is the famous flitch at Dunmow
known to have been claimed?

00:09:50.860 --> 00:09:52.740
I'm going to say really low,
like four or five or something like that.

00:09:52.820 --> 00:09:53.680
MATT: Fifteen.

00:09:53.840 --> 00:09:55.440
I'm going to split the difference and say nine.

00:09:55.580 --> 00:09:57.200
I'm going to give Gary the point. Six.

00:09:57.260 --> 00:09:58.020
Really.

00:09:58.100 --> 00:09:58.940
Six that we know about. [DING]

00:09:59.020 --> 00:10:00.200
When was it repealed?

00:10:00.860 --> 00:10:06.040
Um, it sort of fell apart in the 15th, 16th century
dissolution of the monasteries around then.

00:10:06.280 --> 00:10:07.560
GARY: Okay, yeah.

00:10:07.800 --> 00:10:11.080
But it was revived in Victorian times, of course.

00:10:11.240 --> 00:10:14.640
Natch. In their quest for anything Ye Olde
to legitimize everything.

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:15.600
TOM and CHRIS: Yup.

00:10:15.760 --> 00:10:16.900
Can you still do it now?

00:10:17.160 --> 00:10:18.060
I'm going to say...

00:10:18.220 --> 00:10:19.140
Probably.

00:10:19.280 --> 00:10:22.660
I don't know, maybe if you pay for the bacon
or something like that, I can see.

00:10:22.760 --> 00:10:24.720
I could just go to the butcher's and pay for bacon.

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:26.220
No, but you know what I mean.

00:10:26.220 --> 00:10:28.700
You can have the ceremony,
but you must purchase a flitch of bacon.

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:29.720
CHRIS: Yeah.

00:10:29.860 --> 00:10:32.160
You can actually go every four years.

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:34.760
Once every four years, every leap year in Dunmow,

00:10:34.760 --> 00:10:37.320
there is the Dunmow Flitch Trials Committee...

00:10:37.500 --> 00:10:38.380
Like it.

00:10:38.500 --> 00:10:39.920
TOM: Complete with jury.

00:10:40.160 --> 00:10:41.900
Next one's in 2016.

00:10:42.100 --> 00:10:45.020
If it was me, I would be trying to plan my wedding then,
so I could claim the bacon.

00:10:45.160 --> 00:10:46.120
To land it on...

00:10:47.080 --> 00:10:49.960
I've now looked up the official one here...

00:10:49.960 --> 00:10:52.580
The next one will be on the 9th of July, 2016,

00:10:52.580 --> 00:10:55.500
because there is a website, of course,
for the Dunmow Flitch Trials.

00:10:55.680 --> 00:10:56.620
GARY: Natch.
CHRIS: Yeah.

00:10:57.080 --> 00:11:02.120
There is a list of the successful claimants
going all the way back to 1445...

00:11:02.340 --> 00:11:03.400
Good work.

00:11:03.800 --> 00:11:08.840
And they've also awarded flitches to a couple of couples.
Who might that be?

00:11:09.740 --> 00:11:11.240
Charles and Diana.

00:11:11.380 --> 00:11:12.800
Point. [DING] In fact...

00:11:14.200 --> 00:11:16.600
Charles and Diana got the flitch...

00:11:16.600 --> 00:11:19.300
Prince Andrew and Sarah Ferguson
just got a copy of the Flitch Oath.

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:21.160
What's the Flitch Oath?

00:11:21.160 --> 00:11:24.820
'I pledge allegiance to this bacon
what you have given me. Ta.'

00:11:25.380 --> 00:11:29.340
'In a twelve months time and a day /
Repented not in thought in any way'.

00:11:29.540 --> 00:11:30.420
Ohhh.

00:11:30.540 --> 00:11:32.440
Which is actually a fairly strong oath to take.

00:11:32.540 --> 00:11:33.600
GARY: Yeah, that is, yeah.

00:11:33.720 --> 00:11:35.760
You've never, ever wandered at any point
during that year and a day.

00:11:35.900 --> 00:11:38.860
Don't forget the medieval fear of Hell and Church
and damnation and everything else there, yeah.

00:11:38.980 --> 00:11:41.640
Yes. That's actually a serious oath to take.

00:11:42.200 --> 00:11:46.160
So you can still do it, if any one of us
got married at some point in 2015...

00:11:46.160 --> 00:11:48.260
And that is about all there is to it.

00:11:48.260 --> 00:11:51.500
So it does continue.
They have patrons who are providing the bacon,

00:11:51.500 --> 00:11:54.040
and making sure it continues,
and is one of these ridiculous —

00:11:54.200 --> 00:11:55.700
Bacon patrons!

00:11:55.840 --> 00:11:56.960
We salute you, butchers!

00:11:57.100 --> 00:11:59.220
...ridiculous British traditions
that continues on to this day.

00:11:59.580 --> 00:12:00.340
Good!

00:12:00.520 --> 00:12:02.280
At the end of the show,
congratulations Gary, you win...

00:12:02.460 --> 00:12:03.640
YES!

00:12:03.880 --> 00:12:07.720
You win a gift certificate for the shop
owned by a Treasure Hunt skyrunner,

00:12:07.720 --> 00:12:11.440
which sells small rodents, pests that live in hair,
and adult magazines.

00:12:12.320 --> 00:12:14.960
It's Mice, Lice and Vice by Anneka Rice.

00:12:15.640 --> 00:12:17.800
So enjoy that gift certificate.

00:12:17.800 --> 00:12:20.300
I'm starting to run out of these jokes,
do suggest some more...

00:12:20.440 --> 00:12:22.520
That one was good! It just built and built and built.

00:12:22.520 --> 00:12:23.780
So no, no. Respect.

00:12:24.260 --> 00:12:26.920
TOM: In the meantime, thank you to Chris Joel...

00:12:26.920 --> 00:12:28.400
TOM: To Gary Brannan...

00:12:28.400 --> 00:12:29.160
TOM: To Matt Gray...
MATT: Bye!

00:12:29.260 --> 00:12:31.000
I've been Tom Scott.
We'll see you next time.

00:12:32.240 --> 00:12:35.840
Today's show is sponsored by
the new STI board game...

00:12:36.260 --> 00:12:37.780
Hungry Hungry Herpes.

00:12:38.920 --> 00:12:43.440
...It's the tune that Blur did
that landed on Mars with Beagle 2.

00:12:43.560 --> 00:12:45.440
It didn't land — well, it did land!

00:12:45.640 --> 00:12:46.360
It landed!

00:12:46.540 --> 00:12:49.380
It landed at high velocity! Downwards.

00:12:49.540 --> 00:12:52.160
I know there's a thin line
between 'landing' and 'impact', but...

00:12:52.160 --> 00:12:54.340
I tell that to the wife all the time.

00:12:55.340 --> 00:12:58.120
The one thing I do know about Wolverhampton,
they've got battered chips.

00:12:58.760 --> 00:12:59.820
Oooh.

00:13:00.120 --> 00:13:00.980
Eh?

00:13:01.280 --> 00:13:02.380
Two very different reactions there.

00:13:02.380 --> 00:13:03.560
I went,
[Disappointed] 'Ohh.'

00:13:03.560 --> 00:13:05.240
Matt went, 'Yep, yep, I'll have that.'

00:13:05.360 --> 00:13:07.200
Yeah. I'm with Matt on this.

